Z||000013||Steve Normington||07-10-2001||12:43 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>could someone possibly explain the differances between a custom car and a modded car again? I saw it once but dont feel like hunting the thread up?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Custom car: Changes to your own car or changes that you like to someone else's car.<BR>Modded car: Changes to someone else's car that you don't like (domestic).<BR>Lumped car: Changes to someone else's car that you don't like (european import).<BR>Riced car: Changes to someone else's car that you don't like (asian import).<P>||||reg||1
Z||000014||baldlobo||07-10-2001||01:28 AM||||nuke the windows dir, and reinstall windows. Dos will take several hours to delete the windows dir, so it's better to do it before you go to bed.||||reg||1
Z||000015||SteveJ||07-10-2001||10:17 AM||||Heat around the processor like Formula88<BR>said. This can also be a bad memory chip. Most software problems, as in from the operating system, result in a lockup rather than a reboot.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Formula88||07-10-2001||10:39 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Galen:<BR><B>I wouldn't doubt its rather hot.. my room gets really hot. :/ I guess I'm gonna hafta clean out around the tower some hehe<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That could be it.  It's recommended that you don't run a PC in a room warmer than 85*F, cuz the air's just too warm to cool the PC efficiently.  Depending on how warm your room is, you may want to invest in some extra cooling fans for your PC.  An oversize case fan for intake and exhaust are only a few dollars each and can make a big difference.  CPU coolers get more expensive, but are worth it if you have a high-end processor.  Heat can eventually destroy the CPU, if it gets hot enough.  If your mobo supports it, I highly suggest running health monitoring software to see how hot the cpu and mobo are getting.  My CPU would average only 40-45*C in winter, but in the summer, with my apartment at about 78*F, it gets up to 60*-70*C at the CPU.  Ambient temp inside the case runs up to about 85*-90*F, and that's with extra cooling fans installed.<p>[This message has been edited by Formula88 (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-10-2001||01:31 PM||||Galen,<P>click start. go to run. type MSCONFIG, click on your startup, check the list of programs, remove realplayer from there by removing the check, reboot and try that. <BR>I had a customer have this problem before.<BR>If it still does it, check over the rest and see if you can figure out what one is causing your problems.  Icq me if you need help (118193458)<P>Also, if your box was overheating, worse I ever saw happen was bluescreens, and I work with computers 14 hrs a day.<P><BR>PS.. Norton is crap! It only slows your machine down.<BR>Need a good Antivirus program? <A HREF="http://www.grisoft.com" TARGET=_blank>www.grisoft.com</A>  has a "FREE"  program you can download, why pay peter norton $40+ to takeover your machine?<BR>Never reinstall windows over windows, you end up with duplicate files on top of the same problem you origionally had.<P>Good luck!<P><p>[This message has been edited by isthiswhereiputausername? (edited 07-10-2001).]||||reg||1
